<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">webclient</ansi>

In the web client, the panels either side of the story show your world
and your company. Everything they show, a command shows too, and every
button in them just types a command for you.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The left column: the world</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Time and Date</ansi>, then the <ansi fg="yellow">Map</ansi>, then the <ansi fg="yellow">Room</ansi> you stand in (its exits
  and who's here), and the <ansi fg="yellow">Tutorial</ansi> while you're taking the course.
  The Time and Date sky also shows the weather where you are
  (<ansi fg="command">help weather</ansi>).

  On the map, a small icon in a room's corner marks what it provides:
  fresh water, forage, shelter, and what you can gather. Hover for names;
  the settings button turns the markers off (<ansi fg="command">help resources</ansi>). The Room Info
  panel lists the current room's resources as badges under its name. A
  gathering resource that has been picked clean has an empty-basket mark
  on the map and is dimmed in the panel (<ansi fg="command">help gathering</ansi>).

  You stand on the map as your class, facing the way you walked, with a
  gold ring under you. A small shield with a number shows how many of
  your company travel with you, your party's members show as their
  classes, and your camp (and your party's) shows as a tent with its
  fire. Rooms are terrain tiles with landmarks, walls where there is no
  exit, and fog at the edge of what you know. The settings button has
  Style (Tiles or Classic), Sprites, Camps and Day/night switches
  (<ansi fg="command">help worldmap</ansi>). Click a room on the map to walk there (<ansi fg="command">help walkto</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The right column: the dock</ansi>

  Always at the top: your health and mana, a line for each companion
  (health, and mana for one who casts; one not with you is dimmed; a
  fallen one shows how long is left to raise them), and one line of
  warnings when someone is hungry, thirsty, tired, cold, or the load is
  heavy.

  Below it, the tabs:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Character</ansi>   Overview (your name, class, level, stats, experience, and
              gold), Gear (what you wear and carry, and its weight),
              Skills (each trained skill with what it does, then what
              your abilities and company can do), Quests, Effects, and
              Pet when you have one.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Company</ansi>     Status (formation, where you click a member and then a cell to
              move or swap them, and each member), Inventory (everyone's
              gear, your horses, and the cargo), Camp, Chronicle (the
              company's deeds in sentences, newest first, with a filter by
              kind, and what the towns say of you; help chronicle and
              help townsfolk), Opinions (what each companion likes,
              dislikes and has lately said; help opinions), and Bonds
              (how each pair of companions feels, with a bar for each;
              help bonds).
  <ansi fg="yellow">Combat</ansi>      Setup (how your company fights: formation, each member's
              role and target rule, and your company tactics), and the
              Battle view during a battle. A battle also opens as a
              picture, the battle screen (help battlescreen).
  <ansi fg="yellow">Comm</ansi>        what's said around you; a number on the tab counts what
              arrived while you were looking elsewhere.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Bestiary</ansi>    the kinds of creature you have beaten, by zone; tap one for
              what you have learned of it (help bestiary).

  Turn on <ansi fg="yellow">Online</ansi> or <ansi fg="yellow">KillStats</ansi> in Settings and they join as <ansi fg="yellow">Who</ansi>
  and <ansi fg="yellow">Kills</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What the buttons and menus send</ansi>

  Inventory: tap a member's slot, or click cargo gear, to compare or equip a living member; drag cargo onto a member.
  Character > Gear: select a slot, choose a compatible cargo item, review
  Current → After and Equip item. Preview removal then Remove equipment
  returns the exact worn item to cargo. Unavailable choices explain why. Eat, drink and use items from
  shared cargo outside battle. The treasury and optional autoloot control
  are shown here. cargo put and cargo take now explain shared cargo;
  company meal feeds the band. See help equipment and help loot.

  Camp: Make camp, Light fire, Rest, Break camp, and Inn appear only
  when they would work (<ansi fg="command">camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">inn</ansi>); Meal is always there.
  Under the buttons, "Around the fire" shows your companions' latest talk
  (<ansi fg="command">help banter</ansi>). At your camp, Rest duties has a button for each duty
  under each member: Sleep, Watch, Tend, Forage, Cook (and Brew for an Alchemist).
  The pressed one is the member's duty; the buttons lock while a rest runs
  (<ansi fg="command">help camp duties</ansi>).

  Click a stat, a skill, your race, or your points on the Character tab
  to open its help page; Escape closes it and puts you back where you
  were. Menus work from the keyboard too: Enter opens one, the arrow keys
  move, Enter chooses, and Escape closes it.

  Combat: click a member to change their role or target rule (<ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi>)
  or where they stand (<ansi fg="command">formation</ansi>). Under each row are the member's
  battle orders, with an Orders button to add, remove or reorder them
  (<ansi fg="command">help orders</ansi>), between battles. A guardian's menu picks whom it
  guards ("Guard: ..."), and its row says so, marked "(out of reach)" when
  it stands too far from its ward (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>); click Company tactics to set the
  focus or healing threshold (<ansi fg="command">company tactics</ansi>; the row appears once you
  have a company, and during a battle it only shows your saved tactics). Scout appears when
  enemies are here (<ansi fg="command">scout</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The Battle view</ansi>

  While you fight, the Combat tab shows the battle; when it ends, it goes
  back to Setup. If you're looking at another tab when a battle begins,
  a marker on the Combat tab tells you; it never switches tabs for you.
  Under the battle, and after it, a "Last rounds" list keeps the company's
  latest blows: open one to see why it landed or missed (<ansi fg="command">help battlelog</ansi>).

  The enemy group stands above, your company below (or just "You" when
  you fight alone), each front row nearest the middle, the way <ansi fg="command">scout</ansi>
  draws them. Your company shows its health as numbers; each enemy's
  health reads unhurt, scratched, wounded, badly wounded, or near death,
  never numbers, and a dot marks the ones you can reach from your place.
  Under the focus buttons, "Outlook" gives your company's assessment of
  the fight, the same words scout ends with (<ansi fg="command">help assessment</ansi>), and
  under it how the group fights together (<ansi fg="command">help coordination</ansi>).
  In the dark you see only that it's too dark to make them out.
  Lines run from each fighter to whom it strikes; someone outside your
  company that an enemy strikes shows under "Also fighting". Point at a
  fighter, tab to it, or tap it to light it up with its target and
  everyone striking it.

  Below the grids: who has fallen ("Fallen"), the groups
  "Waiting their turn", and the same lines as a list
  ("Tamsin → the captain"). Click a companion to change their role,
  target rule, or place (the battle plays out by these); the full Setup
  is folded under the view. Retreat orders the company out
  (<ansi fg="command">retreat</ansi>, also typed as <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi>), and the view counts down the rounds.

  Above the grids, the Focus buttons call a new focus for this battle
  (<ansi fg="command">company tactics focus</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>): the current one is marked,
  and all of them wait, greyed out, until your company has turned at the
  next round. "saved" goes back to your saved focus.

  A guardian shows whom it guards and how many guards it has left under
  its name ("guards you, 1 guard left").

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">On a phone</ansi>

  A narrow screen shows one view at a time (Game, Map, Here, Company) from
  a bar along the bottom, with a touch bar of directions, Walk to... and
  common commands above the command box. See <ansi fg="command">help mobile</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Arranging it</ansi>

  The button at the top right of the dock opens the tab you're looking
  at in a window of its own; that window's dock button puts it back.
  Drag the dock's title bar to move it to the other side, and drag the
  column's edge to widen it. The client remembers your tabs and layout.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Settings > Reset Layout</ansi> puts everything back where it started.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company inventory</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help scout</ansi>.

Shared cargo and deliberate equipment assignment: <ansi fg="command">help equipment</ansi>.
Pooled gold: <ansi fg="command">help treasury</ansi>. Battle spoils: <ansi fg="command">help loot</ansi>.

Company Inventory shows shared cargo once, plus each member's gear slots (tap or drag to equip). Character Gear previews any member's changes.

Company Status separates active effects, wounds and persistent bonuses.
Character Skills separates trained ranks, automatic combat abilities,
field and camp capabilities, including disabled settings and conditions,
and Company training: each companion's optional skills and training
points, read only (train with company train).
Character Effects shows your effects and wounds with their true duration
and meaning. See <ansi fg="command">help conditions</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">help skills</ansi>.

The Battle view uses current combat positions on narrow ground, with two active columns and any overflow marked Reserve. Setup continues to show your saved formation. See help battlefield.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Keys</ansi>

  With the command box empty, the arrow keys walk, and Enter opens a quick
  menu of what you can do in this room: look, attack, move, get, gather,
  company, and more (<ansi fg="command">help quickmenu</ansi>).
